export const JEWISH_SCRIPTURES = [
  { category: "jewish", scripture: "The Lord is my light and my salvation; whom shall I fear? The Lord is the stronghold of my life; of whom shall I be afraid? — Psalm 27:1", scriptureExplanation: "David's declaration of trust. 'Light' represents guidance and hope; 'stronghold' represents protection. The verse shifts the focus from what we fear to Who we trust." },
  { category: "jewish", scripture: "He has told you, O man, what is good; and what does the Lord require of you but to do justice, and to love kindness, and to walk humbly with your God? — Micah 6:8", scriptureExplanation: "This distills the entire Torah into three actions. It's not about religious complexity — it's about simple, daily choices: fairness, compassion, and humility." },
  { category: "jewish", scripture: "The Lord will fight for you, and you have only to be silent. — Exodus 14:14", scriptureExplanation: "Spoken at the Red Sea, with the Egyptian army behind and the water ahead. Sometimes faith means stopping our frantic efforts and trusting God to act." },
  { category: "jewish", scripture: "Commit your work to the Lord, and your plans will be established. — Proverbs 16:3", scriptureExplanation: "This isn't a promise of automatic success, but an invitation to surrender our plans to God's wisdom. When we do, our path becomes clearer because it's aligned with His purpose." },
  { category: "jewish", scripture: "The Lord is gracious and merciful, slow to anger and abounding in steadfast love. The Lord is good to all, and his mercy is over all that he has made. — Psalm 145:8-9", scriptureExplanation: "This psalm celebrates God's universal goodness. His mercy isn't earned or limited — it extends to all creation, a reminder that no one is beyond His care." },
  { category: "jewish", scripture: "Do not be afraid, for I am with you; do not be dismayed, for I am your God; I will strengthen you, I will help you, I will uphold you with my righteous right hand. — Isaiah 41:10", scriptureExplanation: "Spoken to a fearful Israel. Three promises — strength, help, and support — address the three things fear takes from us: energy, ability, and stability." },
  { category: "jewish", scripture: "It is well with the man who deals generously and lends; who conducts his affairs with justice. — Psalm 112:5", scriptureExplanation: "Generosity and justice are paired here. The psalmist connects how we treat others with our own well-being. Living generously leads to inner peace." },
  { category: "jewish", scripture: "The Lord redeems the life of his servants; none of those who take refuge in him will be condemned. — Psalm 34:22", scriptureExplanation: "Redemption in the Tanakh means rescue from trouble. Taking refuge in God is an active choice — a deliberate turning toward Him as our safety." },
  { category: "jewish", scripture: "Behold, how good and pleasant it is when brothers dwell in unity! — Psalm 133:1", scriptureExplanation: "This psalm celebrates community. Unity isn't just agreement — it's the shared life that comes from walking together in faith. It's described as both 'good' and 'pleasant.'" },
  { category: "jewish", scripture: "You shall love your neighbor as yourself. — Leviticus 19:18", scriptureExplanation: "This command, later called the second greatest commandment, assumes that we already know how to love ourselves. It asks us to extend that same care to others." },
  { category: "jewish", scripture: "In the beginning, God created the heavens and the earth. — Genesis 1:1", scriptureExplanation: "The opening of the Torah establishes that everything begins with God. Creation is not random — it is intentional, ordered, and good. This verse grounds all of Jewish faith in God as Creator." },
  { category: "jewish", scripture: "And God saw everything that he had made, and behold, it was very good. — Genesis 1:31", scriptureExplanation: "God's assessment of creation is 'very good.' The material world is not evil or secondary — it is God's handiwork, declared excellent. This affirms the goodness of embodied life." },
  { category: "jewish", scripture: "So God created man in his own image, in the image of God he created him; male and female he created them. — Genesis 1:27", scriptureExplanation: "Every human being bears God's image. This is the foundation of human dignity in Jewish thought — worth is not earned but given by the Creator." },
  { category: "jewish", scripture: "And I will establish my covenant between me and you and your offspring after you throughout their generations for an everlasting covenant. — Genesis 17:7", scriptureExplanation: "God's covenant with Abraham is everlasting. It extends beyond one generation — God's commitment is to a people, not just an individual. Covenant is communal." },
  { category: "jewish", scripture: "But the Lord said to Abraham, 'Why did Sarah laugh and say, Shall I indeed bear a child, now that I am old? Is anything too hard for the Lord?' — Genesis 18:13-14", scriptureExplanation: "God's rhetorical question challenges our limited expectations. What we call impossible, God calls opportunity. Faith begins where human calculation ends." },
  { category: "jewish", scripture: "I am the Lord your God, who brought you out of the land of Egypt, out of the house of bondage. — Exodus 20:2", scriptureExplanation: "The preface to the Ten Commandments grounds all of Jewish ethics in liberation. God's commands come from a God who frees — obedience is a response to redemption, not a path to it." },
  { category: "jewish", scripture: "You shall have no other gods before me. — Exodus 20:3", scriptureExplanation: "The first commandment establishes exclusive devotion. God is not one option among many — He is the only God, and our worship belongs to Him alone." },
  { category: "jewish", scripture: "You shall not take the name of the Lord your God in vain, for the Lord will not hold him guiltless who takes his name in vain. — Exodus 20:7", scriptureExplanation: "God's name is sacred. Words matter — how we speak of God reflects how we regard Him. This command protects reverence in a world of casual speech." },
  { category: "jewish", scripture: "Remember the Sabbath day, to keep it holy. — Exodus 20:8", scriptureExplanation: "Sabbath is a gift of rest. God commands rest, not as a burden but as a boundary — protecting us from the tyranny of endless work. Holiness includes ceasing." },
  { category: "jewish", scripture: "Honor your father and your mother, that your days may be long in the land that the Lord your God is giving you. — Exodus 20:12", scriptureExplanation: "Honoring parents is the only commandment with a promise. Family respect is the foundation of a stable society — and it comes with the blessing of longevity." },
  { category: "jewish", scripture: "You shall not murder. — Exodus 20:13", scriptureExplanation: "Life is sacred. This commandment protects human life because every person bears God's image. The prohibition is absolute — life is not ours to take." },
  { category: "jewish", scripture: "You shall not steal. — Exodus 20:15", scriptureExplanation: "Property rights are protected by God. Taking what belongs to another violates not just law but justice — God cares about honest dealing." },
  { category: "jewish", scripture: "You shall not bear false witness against your neighbor. — Exodus 20:16", scriptureExplanation: "Truth matters in community. False testimony destroys lives and justice — honesty is not optional but foundational to a functioning society." },
  { category: "jewish", scripture: "You shall not covet your neighbor's house; you shall not covet your neighbor's wife, or his male servant, or his female servant, or his ox, or his donkey, or anything that is your neighbor's. — Exodus 20:17", scriptureExplanation: "Coveting is the root of other sins. The last commandment addresses the heart — desire precedes action. Inner contentment is the foundation of outer righteousness." },
  { category: "jewish", scripture: "Hear, O Israel: The Lord our God, the Lord is one. You shall love the Lord your God with all your heart and with all your soul and with all your might. — Deuteronomy 6:4-5", scriptureExplanation: "The Shema is the central prayer of Judaism. It declares God's oneness and calls for total love — heart, soul, and might. Devotion is meant to be wholehearted." },
  { category: "jewish", scripture: "And these words that I command you today shall be on your heart. You shall teach them diligently to your children. — Deuteronomy 6:6-7a", scriptureExplanation: "Faith is transmitted. God's words are to be on our hearts and in our children's ears. Transmission is both personal and generational — we teach what we believe." },
  { category: "jewish", scripture: "You shall bind them as a sign on your hand, and they shall be as frontlets between your eyes. — Deuteronomy 6:8", scriptureExplanation: "God's word is to be visible and constant. The commandments shape what we do (hand) and what we see (eyes) — they are meant to guide every action and thought." },
  { category: "jewish", scripture: "You shall write them on the doorposts of your house and on your gates. — Deuteronomy 6:9", scriptureExplanation: "Faith marks our spaces. The home is not separate from God — His word is written on its very structure. Sacred living transforms ordinary spaces." },
  { category: "jewish", scripture: "You shall be holy, for I the Lord your God am holy. — Leviticus 19:2", scriptureExplanation: "Holiness is not just for priests — it's for everyone. The call to be holy echoes God's own holiness. We are to reflect the character of the One who called us." },
  { category: "jewish", scripture: "You shall not oppress your neighbor or rob him. The wages of a hired worker shall not remain with you all night until the morning. — Leviticus 19:13", scriptureExplanation: "Justice is practical. Paying workers on time is a matter of holiness — God cares about economic fairness. Ethics and worship are inseparable." },
  { category: "jewish", scripture: "You shall not take vengeance or bear a grudge against the sons of your own people, but you shall love your neighbor as yourself: I am the Lord. — Leviticus 19:18", scriptureExplanation: "Love replaces vengeance. The command to love neighbor is grounded in God's identity — 'I am the Lord.' Our treatment of others reflects our regard for God." },
  { category: "jewish", scripture: "You shall treat the stranger who sojourns with you as the native among you, and you shall love him as yourself, for you were strangers in the land of Egypt: I am the Lord your God. — Leviticus 19:34", scriptureExplanation: "Love of the stranger is commanded, not suggested. Israel's own experience of oppression is the reason — memory of suffering fuels compassion for others." },
  { category: "jewish", scripture: "You shall do no injustice in court. You shall not be partial to the poor or defer to the great, but in righteousness shall you judge your neighbor. — Leviticus 19:15", scriptureExplanation: "Justice is impartial. Neither wealth nor poverty should sway a judge — righteousness means treating every person equally under the law." },
  { category: "jewish", scripture: "You shall not go around as a slanderer among your people, and you shall not stand up against the life of your neighbor: I am the Lord. — Leviticus 19:16", scriptureExplanation: "Words can kill. Slander and false testimony destroy lives — God takes speech seriously. The command ends with 'I am the Lord' — ethics are theological." },
  { category: "jewish", scripture: "Before the gray head you shall stand up, and you shall honor the face of an old man, and you shall fear your God: I am the Lord. — Leviticus 19:32", scriptureExplanation: "Respect for elders is commanded. Aging is not to be despised but honored — standing for the elderly is an act of reverence for God." },
  { category: "jewish", scripture: "You shall have just balances, just weights, a just ephah, and a just hin: I am the Lord your God, who brought you out of the land of Egypt. — Leviticus 19:36", scriptureExplanation: "Honest commerce is holy. Even weights and measures matter to God — justice in business is as sacred as justice in worship." },
  { category: "jewish", scripture: "The Lord bless you and keep you; the Lord make his face to shine upon you and be gracious to you; the Lord lift up his countenance upon you and give you peace. — Numbers 6:24-26", scriptureExplanation: "The Aaronic blessing. God's face shining represents His favor — peace comes from living in the light of His smile. This is the oldest known biblical text." },
  { category: "jewish", scripture: "The Lord is slow to anger and abounding in steadfast love, forgiving iniquity and transgression. — Numbers 14:18a", scriptureExplanation: "God's patience is not weakness. He is slow to anger — but He is also just. His love and His justice are held together, never in conflict." },
  { category: "jewish", scripture: "But truly, as I live, and as all the earth shall be filled with the glory of the Lord. — Numbers 14:21", scriptureExplanation: "God's glory will fill the earth. This is not a possibility but a certainty — the whole world will one day reflect His splendor. Hope is cosmic." },
  { category: "jewish", scripture: "Not by your sword or by your bow. — Joshua 24:12a", scriptureExplanation: "God's victories are not always by human strength. Sometimes He wins without our weapons — reminding us that the battle is His, not ours." },
  { category: "jewish", scripture: "But as for me and my house, we will serve the Lord. — Joshua 24:15b", scriptureExplanation: "Joshua's declaration of commitment. He doesn't demand — he decides. Personal conviction leads the household. Faith starts with a personal choice." },
  { category: "jewish", scripture: "The Lord is near to all who call on him, to all who call on him in truth. He fulfills the desire of those who fear him; he also hears their cry and saves them. — Psalm 145:18-19", scriptureExplanation: "God's nearness is tied to calling. He responds to honest prayer — not performance, but truth. Those who revere Him find their deepest desires met." },
  { category: "jewish", scripture: "The Lord is my rock and my fortress and my deliverer, my God, my rock, in whom I take refuge, my shield, and the horn of my salvation, my stronghold. — Psalm 18:2", scriptureExplanation: "David piles up metaphors for God's protection. Each image speaks to a different need — God is whatever we require Him to be: rock, fortress, shield, stronghold." },
  { category: "jewish", scripture: "I love you, O Lord, my strength. — Psalm 18:1", scriptureExplanation: "Love precedes strength. David's relationship with God is personal before it is practical — he loves God, then relies on Him. Intimacy is the foundation of trust." },
  { category: "jewish", scripture: "As for God, his way is perfect; the word of the Lord proves true; he is a shield for all those who take refuge in him. — Psalm 18:30", scriptureExplanation: "God's way is perfect even when ours isn't. His word is tested and true — a reliable shield for those who trust. We can lean on what He has said." },
  { category: "jewish", scripture: "You make known to me the path of life; in your presence there is fullness of joy; at your right hand are pleasures forevermore. — Psalm 16:11", scriptureExplanation: "Joy is found in God's presence, not in His gifts. Fullness — not partial, not temporary — is what He offers. The path of life leads to joy." },
  { category: "jewish", scripture: "I have set the Lord always before me; because he is at my right hand, I shall not be shaken. — Psalm 16:8", scriptureExplanation: "David's secret to stability: constant awareness of God. Keeping God before us prevents us from being shaken by what's around us. Focus brings stability." },
  { category: "jewish", scripture: "The heavens declare the glory of God, and the sky above proclaims his handiwork. — Psalm 19:1", scriptureExplanation: "Creation speaks. God's glory is visible to anyone who looks up — the sky itself is a sermon. Revelation is not just in books but in the stars." },
  { category: "jewish", scripture: "The law of the Lord is perfect, reviving the soul; the testimony of the Lord is sure, making wise the simple. — Psalm 19:7", scriptureExplanation: "God's word revives and guides. It's not a burden but a life-giving force — perfect, sure, and wise. Torah is a gift, not a weight." },
  { category: "jewish", scripture: "The precepts of the Lord are right, rejoicing the heart; the commandment of the Lord is pure, enlightening the eyes. — Psalm 19:8", scriptureExplanation: "God's commands bring joy and clarity. They don't restrict — they enlighten. Obedience is not grim duty but a source of delight." },
  { category: "jewish", scripture: "The fear of the Lord is clean, enduring forever; the rules of the Lord are true, and righteous altogether. — Psalm 19:9", scriptureExplanation: "Reverence is clean and eternal. God's rules are true and righteous — not arbitrary but good. The fear of the Lord purifies, it doesn't terrorize." },
  { category: "jewish", scripture: "More to be desired are they than gold, even much fine gold; sweeter also than honey and drippings of the honeycomb. — Psalm 19:10", scriptureExplanation: "God's word is more valuable than wealth and sweeter than honey. The psalmist's appetite for Torah is passionate — it's not duty but delight." },
  { category: "jewish", scripture: "Let the words of my mouth and the meditation of my heart be acceptable in your sight, O Lord, my rock and my redeemer. — Psalm 19:14", scriptureExplanation: "David prays for both outward words and inward thoughts. Acceptability starts within — the heart's meditation shapes the mouth's words." },
  { category: "jewish", scripture: "The Lord is my shepherd; I shall not want. He makes me lie down in green pastures. He leads me beside still waters. He restores my soul. — Psalm 23:1-3", scriptureExplanation: "God is a personal shepherd. He leads, feeds, and restores — not a distant deity but an intimate guide. Contentment comes from the Shepherd, not from circumstances." },
  { category: "jewish", scripture: "Even though I walk through the valley of the shadow of death, I will fear no evil, for you are with me; your rod and your staff, they comfort me. — Psalm 23:4", scriptureExplanation: "The valley is not avoided — it's walked through. But the Shepherd's presence transforms fear into comfort. We don't face darkness alone." },
  { category: "jewish", scripture: "Surely goodness and mercy shall follow me all the days of my life, and I shall dwell in the house of the Lord forever. — Psalm 23:6", scriptureExplanation: "Goodness and mercy pursue us — the Hebrew verb implies active chasing. God's grace runs after us, not away from us. The destination is His house." },
  { category: "jewish", scripture: "One thing have I asked of the Lord, that will I seek after: that I may dwell in the house of the Lord all the days of my life, to gaze upon the beauty of the Lord. — Psalm 27:4", scriptureExplanation: "David's single desire: God's presence. When we seek one thing, everything else finds its place. Focus simplifies life and clarifies priorities." },
  { category: "jewish", scripture: "For in the day of trouble he will keep me safe in his dwelling; he will hide me in the shelter of his tabernacle and set me high upon a rock. — Psalm 27:5", scriptureExplanation: "God's protection is both hiding and elevating. He conceals us from danger and lifts us above it — both are expressions of His care." },
  { category: "jewish", scripture: "Wait for the Lord; be strong, and let your heart take courage; wait for the Lord! — Psalm 27:14", scriptureExplanation: "Courage and waiting are linked. It takes bravery to wait patiently — to trust God's timing instead of forcing our own. Waiting is active, not passive." },
  { category: "jewish", scripture: "The Lord is my light and my salvation; whom shall I fear? — Psalm 27:1a", scriptureExplanation: "Light dispels fear. When God illuminates our path, the darkness loses its power to terrify. Fear is answered by God's presence, not by our courage." },
  { category: "jewish", scripture: "But you, O Lord, are a shield about me, my glory, and the lifter of my head. — Psalm 3:3", scriptureExplanation: "God restores dignity. When circumstances bow our heads, God lifts them — He is the lifter, not the weight. Shame is replaced by honor." },
  { category: "jewish", scripture: "I lay down and slept; I woke again, for the Lord sustained me. — Psalm 3:5", scriptureExplanation: "David slept in the middle of danger. Trust enabled rest — and waking was proof of God's sustaining power. Peace enables sleep." },
  { category: "jewish", scripture: "In peace I will both lie down and sleep; for you alone, O Lord, make me dwell in safety. — Psalm 4:8", scriptureExplanation: "True safety comes from the Lord, not from circumstances. Peace that allows sleep is a gift from God — security is His to give." },
  { category: "jewish", scripture: "But let all who take refuge in you rejoice; let them ever sing for joy, and spread your protection over them. — Psalm 5:11", scriptureExplanation: "Joy is the natural response to God's protection. Taking refuge in God leads not to fear but to celebration — safety and joy are connected." },
  { category: "jewish", scripture: "The Lord is in his holy temple; the Lord's throne is in heaven; his eyes see, his eyelids test the children of man. — Psalm 11:4", scriptureExplanation: "God is not absent — He is enthroned and watching. Nothing escapes His notice; His perspective is from eternity. He sees everything." },
  { category: "jewish", scripture: "But you, O Lord, are a shield about me, my glory, and the lifter of my head. — Psalm 3:3 (alt.)", scriptureExplanation: "God lifts what circumstances have bowed. He is both shield (protection) and glory (honor) — He defends and dignifies at once." },
  { category: "jewish", scripture: "God is our refuge and strength, a very present help in trouble. — Psalm 46:1", scriptureExplanation: "God is both refuge (protection) and strength (power). He is 'very present' — not distant, not delayed, but here. Help is immediate." },
  { category: "jewish", scripture: "Therefore we will not fear though the earth gives way, though the mountains be moved into the heart of the sea. — Psalm 46:2", scriptureExplanation: "Fearlessness is rooted in God's presence, not in stable circumstances. Even if the world shakes, we stand — because God is our refuge." },
  { category: "jewish", scripture: "There is a river whose streams make glad the city of God, the holy habitation of the Most High. — Psalm 46:4", scriptureExplanation: "God's presence is like a river — refreshing, life-giving, and constant. It brings gladness, not anxiety. The city of God is a place of joy." },
  { category: "jewish", scripture: "The Lord of hosts is with us; the God of Jacob is our fortress. — Psalm 46:7", scriptureExplanation: "God's presence is our defense. The Lord of angel armies is with us — no force can overwhelm His protection. He is both with us and for us." },
  { category: "jewish", scripture: "Be still, and know that I am God. — Psalm 46:10a", scriptureExplanation: "Stillness is the path to knowing. We stop striving so we can recognize — God's sovereignty is revealed in our surrender. Quietness is not emptiness." },
  { category: "jewish", scripture: "Great is the Lord, and greatly to be praised, in the city of our God, his holy mountain. — Psalm 48:1", scriptureExplanation: "God's greatness demands praise. Worship is the fitting response to who God is — it's not optional, it's appropriate. Praise matches His greatness." },
  { category: "jewish", scripture: "Create in me a clean heart, O God, and renew a right spirit within me. — Psalm 51:10", scriptureExplanation: "David's prayer after his deepest failure. He doesn't ask for a second chance — he asks for a new heart. Transformation is God's work, not self-improvement." },
  { category: "jewish", scripture: "Cast me not away from your presence, and take not your Holy Spirit from me. — Psalm 51:11", scriptureExplanation: "David's greatest fear was losing God's presence. More than consequences, he feared separation — intimacy was his highest value." },
  { category: "jewish", scripture: "Restore to me the joy of your salvation, and uphold me with a willing spirit. — Psalm 51:12", scriptureExplanation: "Joy in salvation can be lost. David asks for its return — not a new salvation, but renewed joy in what God has done. Joy is recoverable." },
  { category: "jewish", scripture: "The sacrifices of God are a broken spirit; a broken and contrite heart, O God, you will not despise. — Psalm 51:17", scriptureExplanation: "God values humility over ritual. A broken heart is not rejected — it's the very thing God receives. Contrition is the true sacrifice." },
  { category: "jewish", scripture: "When I am afraid, I put my trust in you. — Psalm 56:3", scriptureExplanation: "Fear and trust coexist. David doesn't wait for fear to leave before trusting — he trusts in the middle of fear. Trust is a choice, not a feeling." },
  { category: "jewish", scripture: "In God, whose word I praise, in God I trust; I shall not be afraid. What can flesh do to me? — Psalm 56:4", scriptureExplanation: "Trust displaces fear. When we trust God's word, human threats lose their power to terrify. The mortal can't ultimately harm us." },
  { category: "jewish", scripture: "You have kept count of my tossings; put my tears in your bottle. Are they not in your book? — Psalm 56:8", scriptureExplanation: "God collects our tears. No sorrow is wasted — every grief is noticed, recorded, and valued by God. He keeps track of our pain." },
  { category: "jewish", scripture: "Then I shall not be put to shame, having my eyes on all your commandments. — Psalm 119:6", scriptureExplanation: "Focus on God's commands prevents shame. Obedience is not about earning but about alignment — living in tune with God's design." },
  { category: "jewish", scripture: "I will meditate on your precepts and fix my eyes on your ways. — Psalm 119:15", scriptureExplanation: "Meditation is focused attention. Fixing our eyes on God's ways leads to steady, intentional living. The mind shapes the life." },
  { category: "jewish", scripture: "Your word is a lamp to my feet and a light to my path. — Psalm 119:105", scriptureExplanation: "God's word illuminates the next step. It doesn't show the whole journey — just enough to walk forward in faith. Light is for the path, not the horizon." },
  { category: "jewish", scripture: "The unfolding of your words gives light; it imparts understanding to the simple. — Psalm 119:130", scriptureExplanation: "God's word brings clarity. As it unfolds, light increases — understanding is a gift, not just an achievement. The simple become wise." },
  { category: "jewish", scripture: "Let your steadfast love comfort me according to your promise to your servant. — Psalm 119:76", scriptureExplanation: "Comfort comes from God's love, not from circumstances. His promise is the basis — we rest in what He's committed to, not in what we feel." },
  { category: "jewish", scripture: "I have longed for your salvation, O Lord, and your law is my delight. — Psalm 119:174", scriptureExplanation: "Delight in God's word and longing for His salvation go together. The more we love His word, the more we desire His rescue. Torah and salvation are linked." },
  { category: "jewish", scripture: "Let me live that I may praise you, and let your rules help me. — Psalm 119:175", scriptureExplanation: "Life is for praise. David's prayer for life is tied to worship — existence has purpose when it's directed toward God. Living is praising." },
  { category: "jewish", scripture: "I cry out to the Lord; I plead with the Lord for mercy. — Psalm 142:1", scriptureExplanation: "Honest prayer is raw. David doesn't dress up his cry — he brings his desperation directly to God. Honesty is the heart of prayer." },
  { category: "jewish", scripture: "When my spirit grows faint within me, it is you who watch over my way. — Psalm 142:3a", scriptureExplanation: "Even in exhaustion, God watches. When we can't watch over ourselves, He watches over us. Faintness doesn't end His care." },
  { category: "jewish", scripture: "The Lord upholds all who are falling and raises up all who are bowed down. — Psalm 145:14", scriptureExplanation: "God catches the falling and lifts the bowed. His help is for those who can't help themselves — His strength is for the weak." },
  { category: "jewish", scripture: "The eyes of all look to you, and you give them their food in due season. — Psalm 145:15", scriptureExplanation: "God provides for all creation. Every living thing depends on Him — and He feeds them, each in its time. Provision is universal." },
  { category: "jewish", scripture: "You open your hand; you satisfy the desire of every living thing. — Psalm 145:16", scriptureExplanation: "God's provision is generous. His open hand satisfies — He doesn't give grudgingly but abundantly. Satisfaction comes from His hand." },
  { category: "jewish", scripture: "The Lord is righteous in all his ways and kind in all his works. — Psalm 145:17", scriptureExplanation: "God's actions are both right and kind. Justice and compassion are never in tension — both define everything He does." },
  { category: "jewish", scripture: "He fulfills the desire of those who fear him; he also hears their cry and saves them. — Psalm 145:19", scriptureExplanation: "God hears and acts. Reverence leads to fulfillment — those who honor God find their deepest desires met in Him." },
  { category: "jewish", scripture: "The Lord preserves all who love him, but all the wicked he will destroy. — Psalm 145:20", scriptureExplanation: "God's protection is for those who love Him. Love is both the condition and the connection — relationship secures preservation." },
  { category: "jewish", scripture: "My mouth will speak the praise of the Lord, and let all flesh bless his holy name forever and ever. — Psalm 145:21", scriptureExplanation: "Praise is eternal. David's personal commitment becomes a universal call — all creation is invited to bless God." },
  { category: "jewish", scripture: "Praise the Lord! Praise the Lord, O my soul! — Psalm 146:1", scriptureExplanation: "David commands his own soul to praise. Worship is a choice — we direct our inner being toward God. Praise is not spontaneous; it's intentional." },
  { category: "jewish", scripture: "I will praise the Lord as long as I live; I will sing praises to my God while I have my being. — Psalm 146:2", scriptureExplanation: "Praise is lifelong. As long as we have breath, we have reason to sing — existence itself is a gift to celebrate." },
  { category: "jewish", scripture: "Put not your trust in princes, in a son of man, in whom there is no salvation. — Psalm 146:3", scriptureExplanation: "Human help is limited. Even the most powerful people can't save — only God can. Trust belongs to Him alone." },
  { category: "jewish", scripture: "Blessed is he whose help is the God of Jacob, whose hope is in the Lord his God. — Psalm 146:5", scriptureExplanation: "True blessing comes from God-reliance. Those whose hope is in the Lord are truly blessed — their foundation is unshakable." },
  { category: "jewish", scripture: "The Lord watches over the sojourners; he upholds the widow and the fatherless. — Psalm 146:9", scriptureExplanation: "God's special care is for the vulnerable. He watches over those without protection — His justice is expressed in tenderness." },
  { category: "jewish", scripture: "Praise the Lord! For it is good to sing praises to our God; for it is pleasant, and a song of praise is fitting. — Psalm 147:1", scriptureExplanation: "Praise is both good and pleasant. It's not just duty — it's delight. Worship is the fitting response to God's goodness." },
  { category: "jewish", scripture: "The Lord builds up Jerusalem; he gathers the outcasts of Israel. — Psalm 147:2", scriptureExplanation: "God builds and gathers. He restores what's broken and collects what's scattered — His work is always reuniting." },
  { category: "jewish", scripture: "He heals the brokenhearted and binds up their wounds. — Psalm 147:3", scriptureExplanation: "God is a healer. His care is tender and specific — He binds wounds one by one, not in bulk. Healing is personal." },
  { category: "jewish", scripture: "Great is our Lord, and abundant in power; his understanding is beyond measure. — Psalm 147:5", scriptureExplanation: "God's power and wisdom are limitless. He is great enough to handle anything and wise enough to understand everything." },
  { category: "jewish", scripture: "The Lord lifts up the humble; he casts the wicked to the ground. — Psalm 147:6", scriptureExplanation: "God reverses positions. The humble are lifted; the proud are brought low. His economy is the opposite of the world's." },
  { category: "jewish", scripture: "But the Lord takes pleasure in those who fear him, in those who hope in his steadfast love. — Psalm 147:11", scriptureExplanation: "God's pleasure is in our trust. What He wants from us is not strength but hope — reliance on His love." },
  { category: "jewish", scripture: "Praise the Lord from the heavens; praise him in the heights! — Psalm 148:1", scriptureExplanation: "Praise begins above. The call to worship starts in the heavens — creation itself is invited to lead." },
  { category: "jewish", scripture: "Praise him, sun and moon, praise him, all you shining stars! — Psalm 148:3", scriptureExplanation: "The cosmos praises God. The sun, moon, and stars are called to worship — all creation has a voice." },
  { category: "jewish", scripture: "Let everything that has breath praise the Lord! Praise the Lord! — Psalm 150:6", scriptureExplanation: "The final call: all breath, all praise. Every living thing is invited — worship is the purpose of breath itself." },
  { category: "jewish", scripture: "The fear of the Lord is the beginning of knowledge; fools despise wisdom and instruction. — Proverbs 1:7", scriptureExplanation: "Reverence is the foundation of learning. True knowledge starts with awe of God — not with information, but with worship." },
  { category: "jewish", scripture: "Trust in the Lord with all your heart, and do not lean on your own understanding. — Proverbs 3:5", scriptureExplanation: "Trust replaces self-reliance. Our understanding is limited; God's is not. Leaning on Him is safer than leaning on ourselves." },
  { category: "jewish", scripture: "In all your ways acknowledge him, and he will make straight your paths. — Proverbs 3:6", scriptureExplanation: "Acknowledgment brings direction. When we include God in every decision, our path becomes clear — not because we see it all, but because He does." },
  { category: "jewish", scripture: "Honor the Lord with your wealth and with the firstfruits of all your produce. — Proverbs 3:9", scriptureExplanation: "Generosity honors God. Giving first — not last — shows trust. The firstfruits declare that God comes before our needs." },
  { category: "jewish", scripture: "My son, do not despise the Lord's discipline or be weary of his reproof, for the Lord reproves him whom he loves. — Proverbs 3:11-12", scriptureExplanation: "Discipline is evidence of love. God corrects those He cares about — His reproof is not rejection but investment." },
  { category: "jewish", scripture: "Keep your heart with all vigilance, for from it flow the springs of life. — Proverbs 4:23", scriptureExplanation: "The heart is the source. What we allow into our inner life determines what flows out — guarding the heart is guarding everything." },
  { category: "jewish", scripture: "A gentle tongue is a tree of life, but perverseness in it breaks the spirit. — Proverbs 15:4", scriptureExplanation: "Words can heal or harm. A gentle word gives life; a perverse word destroys. Speech is not neutral — it shapes the spirit." },
  { category: "jewish", scripture: "A glad heart makes a cheerful face, but by sorrow of heart the spirit is crushed. — Proverbs 15:13", scriptureExplanation: "Inner joy shows outwardly. The heart and the face are connected — what we feel inside radiates outside. Joy is visible." },
  { category: "jewish", scripture: "A hot-tempered man stirs up strife, but he who is slow to anger quiets contention. — Proverbs 15:18", scriptureExplanation: "Patience brings peace. The slow-to-anger person doesn't just avoid conflict — they actively calm it. Self-control is contagious." },
  { category: "jewish", scripture: "Without counsel plans fail, but with many advisers they succeed. — Proverbs 15:22", scriptureExplanation: "Wisdom is communal. We need others' input — plans made alone are fragile. Success comes from shared counsel." },
  { category: "jewish", scripture: "The fear of the Lord is a fountain of life, that one may turn away from the snares of death. — Proverbs 14:27", scriptureExplanation: "Reverence gives life. The fear of the Lord is not terror but a fountain — it nourishes and protects, turning us from death." },
  { category: "jewish", scripture: "In all toil there is profit, but mere talk tends only to poverty. — Proverbs 14:23", scriptureExplanation: "Work pays; talk doesn't. Action over words — effort produces results while mere discussion produces nothing. Diligence is rewarded." },
  { category: "jewish", scripture: "Righteousness exalts a nation, but sin is a reproach to any people. — Proverbs 14:34", scriptureExplanation: "Morality is national, not just personal. Righteousness lifts a society; sin drags it down. Ethics have public consequences." },
  { category: "jewish", scripture: "A soft answer turns away wrath, but a harsh word stirs up anger. — Proverbs 15:1", scriptureExplanation: "Response shapes conflict. A gentle reply defuses; a harsh one escalates. The tone of our words determines the temperature of the room." },
  { category: "jewish", scripture: "The name of the Lord is a strong tower; the righteous man runs into it and is safe. — Proverbs 18:10", scriptureExplanation: "God's name is protection. The righteous find safety in who God is — His character is a fortress. Running to Him is running to safety." },
  { category: "jewish", scripture: "Whoever is slow to anger is better than the mighty, and he who rules his spirit than he who takes a city. — Proverbs 16:32", scriptureExplanation: "Self-control is greater than conquest. Ruling your own spirit is harder and more valuable than capturing a city. Inner strength surpasses outer power." },
  { category: "jewish", scripture: "Commit your work to the Lord, and your plans will be established. — Proverbs 16:3", scriptureExplanation: "Surrender brings stability. When we commit our plans to God, they find their footing — not because we're smart, but because He's sovereign." },
  { category: "jewish", scripture: "The lot is cast into the lap, but its every decision is from the Lord. — Proverbs 16:33", scriptureExplanation: "Even chance is under God's control. Nothing is random from His perspective — every outcome is directed by His sovereignty." },
  { category: "jewish", scripture: "A merry heart does good like a medicine, but a broken spirit dries the bones. — Proverbs 17:22", scriptureExplanation: "Joy heals. A glad heart is medicinal — it strengthens the body. Conversely, a crushed spirit drains physical life. Mind and body are connected." },
  { category: "jewish", scripture: "He who has knowledge spares his words, and a man of understanding is of a calm spirit. — Proverbs 17:27", scriptureExplanation: "Restraint shows wisdom. The knowledgeable speak less, not more. Calmness of spirit is a mark of understanding." },
  { category: "jewish", scripture: "Death and life are in the power of the tongue, and those who love it will eat its fruits. — Proverbs 18:21", scriptureExplanation: "Words create and destroy. The tongue can kill or give life — and we will eat the fruit of what we say. Speech is creative power." },
  { category: "jewish", scripture: "Whoever guards his mouth and tongue keeps his soul from troubles. — Proverbs 21:23", scriptureExplanation: "Speech discipline protects the soul. Watching our words prevents trouble — the mouth is a gateway to either peace or chaos." },
  { category: "jewish", scripture: "The horse is made ready for the day of battle, but the victory belongs to the Lord. — Proverbs 21:31", scriptureExplanation: "Preparation and trust go together. We prepare, but God determines the outcome. Both effort and dependence are required." },
  { category: "jewish", scripture: "A good name is to be chosen rather than great riches, and favor is better than silver or gold. — Proverbs 22:1", scriptureExplanation: "Reputation is worth more than wealth. A good name — character — is more valuable than money. Integrity is the true treasure." },
  { category: "jewish", scripture: "Train up a child in the way he should go; even when he is old he will not depart from it. — Proverbs 22:6", scriptureExplanation: "Early formation lasts. Training a child shapes a lifetime — the foundation laid in youth endures into old age. Parenting is formative." },
  { category: "jewish", scripture: "Do not withhold good from those to whom it is due, when it is in your power to do it. — Proverbs 3:27", scriptureExplanation: "Do good when you can. If we have the power to help, we should — delay is a form of denial. Opportunity is a responsibility." },
  { category: "jewish", scripture: "Do not plan evil against your neighbor, who dwells trustingly beside you. — Proverbs 3:29", scriptureExplanation: "Trust is sacred. Our neighbor trusts us, and we must not betray that trust. Community requires mutual safety." },
  { category: "jewish", scripture: "Do not contend with a man for no reason, when he has done you no harm. — Proverbs 3:30", scriptureExplanation: "Don't pick fights. Unnecessary conflict is foolish — if there's no harm, there's no reason to contend. Peace is the default." },
  { category: "jewish", scripture: "Do not envy a man of violence and do not choose any of his ways. — Proverbs 3:31", scriptureExplanation: "Don't admire the aggressive. Violence may seem powerful, but its path leads to destruction. Choose a different way." },
  { category: "jewish", scripture: "For the devious person is an abomination to the Lord, but the upright are in his confidence. — Proverbs 3:32", scriptureExplanation: "God shares secrets with the upright. Integrity brings intimacy with God — the honest enjoy His confidence." },
  { category: "jewish", scripture: "The Lord's curse is on the house of the wicked, but he blesses the dwelling of the righteous. — Proverbs 3:33", scriptureExplanation: "God's blessing follows righteousness. The home of the upright is blessed — character affects the household." },
  { category: "jewish", scripture: "Toward the scorners he is scornful, but to the humble he gives favor. — Proverbs 3:34", scriptureExplanation: "God matches our posture. The proud get scorn; the humble get grace. Our attitude toward God shapes His response to us." },
  { category: "jewish", scripture: "The wise will inherit honor, but fools get disgrace. — Proverbs 3:35", scriptureExplanation: "Wisdom leads to honor. The wise are respected; the foolish are shamed. Choices have consequences in reputation." },
  { category: "jewish", scripture: "For the Lord gives wisdom; from his mouth come knowledge and understanding. — Proverbs 2:6", scriptureExplanation: "Wisdom is God's gift. It doesn't come from experience alone — it comes from God. He is the source of true knowledge." },
  { category: "jewish", scripture: "He stores up sound wisdom for the upright; he is a shield to those who walk in integrity. — Proverbs 2:7", scriptureExplanation: "God protects the honest. Integrity is both rewarded and defended — God is a shield to those who walk straight." },
  { category: "jewish", scripture: "Then you will understand righteousness and justice and equity, every good path. — Proverbs 2:9", scriptureExplanation: "Wisdom shows the right path. Understanding comes from seeking — and it reveals the way of justice and goodness." },
  { category: "jewish", scripture: "For wisdom will come into your heart, and knowledge will be pleasant to your soul. — Proverbs 2:10", scriptureExplanation: "Wisdom is internalized. It enters the heart and delights the soul — true knowledge is not just information but joy." },
  { category: "jewish", scripture: "Discretion will protect you, and understanding will guard you. — Proverbs 2:11", scriptureExplanation: "Wisdom is protective. Good judgment keeps us from harm — understanding acts as a guard over our decisions." },
  { category: "jewish", scripture: "The beginning of wisdom is this: Get wisdom, and whatever you get, get insight. — Proverbs 4:7", scriptureExplanation: "The first step is to seek wisdom. Above all else, pursue understanding — it's the foundation of everything else." },
  { category: "jewish", scripture: "Prize her highly, and she will exalt you; she will honor you if you embrace her. — Proverbs 4:8", scriptureExplanation: "Wisdom elevates those who value it. Embrace wisdom, and she lifts you up — honor follows those who honor her." },
  { category: "jewish", scripture: "She will place on your head a graceful garland; she will bestow on you a beautiful crown. — Proverbs 4:9", scriptureExplanation: "Wisdom crowns the wise. The reward of wisdom is not just practical but beautiful — it adorns the life it shapes." },
  { category: "jewish", scripture: "Hear, my son, and accept my words, that the years of your life may be many. — Proverbs 4:10", scriptureExplanation: "Listening extends life. Accepting instruction leads to longevity — the wise live longer because they live better." },
  { category: "jewish", scripture: "I have taught you the way of wisdom; I have led you in the paths of uprightness. — Proverbs 4:11", scriptureExplanation: "Wisdom has a path. It's not abstract — it's a way to walk. Instruction leads to upright living." },
  { category: "jewish", scripture: "When you walk, your step will not be hampered, and if you run, you will not stumble. — Proverbs 4:12", scriptureExplanation: "Wisdom gives freedom of movement. The wise walk without hindrance and run without falling — clarity brings confidence." },
  { category: "jewish", scripture: "Keep hold of instruction; do not let go; guard her, for she is your life. — Proverbs 4:13", scriptureExplanation: "Instruction is life itself. Don't let go of wisdom — she is not optional but essential. Guarding wisdom is guarding life." },
  { category: "jewish", scripture: "For they cannot sleep unless they have done wrong; they are robbed of sleep unless they have made someone stumble. — Proverbs 4:16", scriptureExplanation: "Evil is restless. The wicked can't sleep without doing harm — sin is compulsive. Righteousness, by contrast, brings rest." },
  { category: "jewish", scripture: "For the path of the righteous is like the light of dawn, which shines brighter and brighter until full day. — Proverbs 4:18", scriptureExplanation: "Righteousness is progressive. The path of the upright gets brighter — it starts at dawn and grows to full day. Goodness increases." },
  { category: "jewish", scripture: "The way of the wicked is like deep darkness; they do not know over what they will stumble. — Proverbs 4:19", scriptureExplanation: "Wickedness is blind. The wicked walk in darkness and don't even know what trips them — sin obscures its own consequences." },
  { category: "jewish", scripture: "My son, be attentive to my words; incline your ear to my sayings. — Proverbs 4:20", scriptureExplanation: "Listening is active. We must incline our ears — lean in, pay attention. Wisdom comes to those who listen carefully." },
  { category: "jewish", scripture: "Let them not escape from your sight; keep them within your heart. — Proverbs 4:21", scriptureExplanation: "Wisdom must be internalized. Don't let it slip away — keep it in your heart. What's stored inside shapes what comes out." },
  { category: "jewish", scripture: "For they are life to those who find them, and healing to all their flesh. — Proverbs 4:22", scriptureExplanation: "Wisdom brings health. These words are not just spiritual — they bring healing to the body. Mind and body are connected." },
  { category: "jewish", scripture: "Ponder the path of your feet; then all your ways will be sure. — Proverbs 4:26", scriptureExplanation: "Think about where you're going. Reflection on our path leads to sure steps — consideration prevents stumbling." },
  { category: "jewish", scripture: "Do not swerve to the right or to the left; turn your foot away from evil. — Proverbs 4:27", scriptureExplanation: "Stay on the path. Don't wander — keep your feet straight. Avoiding evil requires intentional direction." },
  { category: "jewish", scripture: "For whatever one sows, that will he also reap. — Galatians 6:7b (principle also in Proverbs)", scriptureExplanation: "Actions have consequences. This universal principle — also found in Proverbs — means our choices produce matching results. We harvest what we plant." },
  { category: "jewish", scripture: "The one who sows righteousness will have a sure reward. — Proverbs 11:18b", scriptureExplanation: "Righteousness pays off. The reward is certain — not immediate, but sure. Good living produces good results." },
  { category: "jewish", scripture: "Whoever diligently seeks good seeks favor, but evil comes to him who searches for it. — Proverbs 11:27", scriptureExplanation: "What we seek, we find. Seeking good leads to favor; seeking evil leads to trouble. Our desires shape our destiny." },
  { category: "jewish", scripture: "Whoever trusts in his riches will fall, but the righteous will flourish like a green leaf. — Proverbs 11:28", scriptureExplanation: "Wealth is unstable; righteousness is enduring. Trusting money leads to a fall; trusting God leads to flourishing. The leaf stays green." },
  { category: "jewish", scripture: "Whoever brings blessing will be enriched, and one who waters will himself be watered. — Proverbs 11:25", scriptureExplanation: "Generosity is self-watering. Those who bless others are blessed; those who refresh are refreshed. Giving is receiving." },
  { category: "jewish", scripture: "The desire of the righteous ends only in good, the expectation of the wicked in wrath. — Proverbs 11:23", scriptureExplanation: "Righteous desires lead to good outcomes. What the righteous want aligns with what God wants — and that alignment produces good." },
  { category: "jewish", scripture: "Like a gold ring in a pig's snout is a beautiful woman without discretion. — Proverbs 11:22", scriptureExplanation: "Beauty without character is wasted. Discretion is more valuable than appearance — character adorns beauty, not the other way around." },
  { category: "jewish", scripture: "Where there is no guidance, a people falls, but in an abundance of counselors there is safety. — Proverbs 11:14", scriptureExplanation: "Counsel prevents collapse. Without guidance, people fall; with many advisors, they're safe. Wisdom is communal." },
  { category: "jewish", scripture: "Whoever belittles his neighbor lacks sense, but a man of understanding remains silent. — Proverbs 11:12", scriptureExplanation: "Silence is wise. Mocking others shows foolishness; understanding keeps quiet. Sometimes the wisest word is none." },
  { category: "jewish", scripture: "By the blessing of the upright a city is exalted, but by the mouth of the wicked it is overthrown. — Proverbs 11:11", scriptureExplanation: "Words shape communities. The blessing of the righteous lifts a city; the words of the wicked destroy it. Speech has civic impact." },
  { category: "jewish", scripture: "When the righteous prosper, the city rejoices; and when the wicked perish, there are shouts of joy. — Proverbs 11:10", scriptureExplanation: "Righteousness brings public joy. Good people make the whole community glad — character has a ripple effect." },
  { category: "jewish", scripture: "With his mouth the godless man would destroy his neighbor, but by knowledge the righteous are delivered. — Proverbs 11:9", scriptureExplanation: "Knowledge rescues. Words can destroy, but understanding saves — the righteous are delivered through what they know." },
  { category: "jewish", scripture: "The righteous is delivered from trouble, and the wicked walks into it instead. — Proverbs 11:8", scriptureExplanation: "Trouble finds the wicked; the righteous escape it. Not because they're lucky, but because wisdom steers them away from danger." },
  { category: "jewish", scripture: "When the wicked dies, his hopes perish, and the expectation of the unjust comes to nothing. — Proverbs 11:7", scriptureExplanation: "Wicked hope is empty. Without righteousness, expectations collapse — only the upright have lasting hope." },
  { category: "jewish", scripture: "A gracious woman gets honor, and violent men get riches. — Proverbs 11:16", scriptureExplanation: "Honor and wealth are different currencies. Grace earns honor; violence earns money. The wise choose honor over wealth." },
  { category: "jewish", scripture: "A man who is kind benefits himself, but a cruel man hurts himself. — Proverbs 11:17", scriptureExplanation: "Kindness is self-benefiting. Cruelty is self-destructive. How we treat others returns to us — character is its own reward." },
  { category: "jewish", scripture: "An anxious heart weighs a man down, but a good word makes it glad. — Proverbs 12:25", scriptureExplanation: "Words carry weight. Anxiety depresses; a good word lifts. Our speech can either burden or brighten someone's heart." },
  { category: "jewish", scripture: "The way of a fool is right in his own eyes, but a wise man listens to advice. — Proverbs 12:15", scriptureExplanation: "Fools are self-assured; the wise are teachable. The fool thinks he's always right; the wise person seeks counsel." },
  { category: "jewish", scripture: "The vexation of a fool is known at once, but the prudent ignores an insult. — Proverbs 12:16", scriptureExplanation: "Patience hides offense. The fool reacts instantly; the wise overlook insults. Self-control keeps peace." },
  { category: "jewish", scripture: "Whoever speaks the truth gives honest evidence, but a false witness utters deceit. — Proverbs 12:17", scriptureExplanation: "Truth is reliable. Honest testimony reflects reality; lies distort it. Truth-telling is a form of justice." },
  { category: "jewish", scripture: "There is one whose rash words are like sword thrusts, but the tongue of the wise brings healing. — Proverbs 12:18", scriptureExplanation: "Words wound or heal. Reckless speech cuts like a sword; wise speech mends. The tongue is either a weapon or a remedy." },
  { category: "jewish", scripture: "Truthful lips endure forever, but a lying tongue is but for a moment. — Proverbs 12:19", scriptureExplanation: "Truth lasts; lies don't. Deceit is temporary — it collapses eventually. Honesty endures because it aligns with reality." },
  { category: "jewish", scripture: "Deceit is in the heart of those who devise evil, but those who plan peace have joy. — Proverbs 12:20", scriptureExplanation: "Planning peace brings joy. Evil intentions breed deceit; peace plans produce gladness. What we plot shapes how we feel." },
  { category: "jewish", scripture: "No ill befalls the righteous, but the wicked are filled with trouble. — Proverbs 12:21", scriptureExplanation: "Righteousness provides protection. Not that the righteous never suffer, but trouble doesn't define them — they're shielded from unnecessary harm." },
  { category: "jewish", scripture: "Lying lips are an abomination to the Lord, but those who act faithfully are his delight. — Proverbs 12:22", scriptureExplanation: "God delights in honesty. Truthfulness is not just practical — it's worship. Faithful action brings God joy." },
  { category: "jewish", scripture: "A prudent man conceals knowledge, but the heart of fools proclaims folly. — Proverbs 12:23", scriptureExplanation: "Discretion is wise. The prudent don't broadcast everything; the fool can't stop talking. Restraint is a mark of understanding." },
  { category: "jewish", scripture: "The hand of the diligent will rule, while the slothful will be put to forced labor. — Proverbs 12:24", scriptureExplanation: "Diligence leads to leadership. Hard work elevates; laziness enslaves. Effort determines position." },
  { category: "jewish", scripture: "Anxiety in a man's heart weighs him down, but a good word makes him glad. — Proverbs 12:25 (alt.)", scriptureExplanation: "Encouragement lifts. A well-timed word can transform someone's day — we have the power to lighten heavy hearts." },
  { category: "jewish", scripture: "One righteous is a choice morsel, but the way of the wicked leads them astray. — Proverbs 12:26", scriptureExplanation: "The righteous choose wisely. They're discerning about their path — while the wicked are misled by their own way." },
  { category: "jewish", scripture: "A slothful man does not roast his prey, but the diligent man prizes his possessions. — Proverbs 12:27", scriptureExplanation: "Effort preserves what we have. Laziness loses even what's caught; diligence values and maintains what's earned." },
  { category: "jewish", scripture: "In the path of righteousness is life, and in its pathway there is no death. — Proverbs 12:28", scriptureExplanation: "Righteousness leads to life. The path of the upright is free from death — not literal immortality, but a life-giving direction." },
  { category: "jewish", scripture: "A wise son hears his father's instruction, but a scoffer does not listen to rebuke. — Proverbs 13:1", scriptureExplanation: "Teachable vs. scornful. The wise child listens; the mocker rejects correction. Receptivity to instruction is a sign of wisdom." },
  { category: "jewish", scripture: "From the fruit of his mouth a man eats what is good, but the desire of the treacherous is violence. — Proverbs 13:2", scriptureExplanation: "Words produce fruit. Good speech feeds the speaker; treacherous desires consume the wicked. We eat what we speak." },
  { category: "jewish", scripture: "Whoever guards his mouth preserves his life; he who opens wide his lips comes to ruin. — Proverbs 13:3", scriptureExplanation: "Speech discipline is self-preservation. Loose lips destroy; guarded words protect. The mouth is a gateway to life or ruin." },
  { category: "jewish", scripture: "The soul of the sluggard craves and gets nothing, while the soul of the diligent is richly supplied. — Proverbs 13:4", scriptureExplanation: "Desire without work is empty. The lazy want but don't get; the diligent are satisfied. Effort fulfills craving." },
  { category: "jewish", scripture: "A righteous man hates falsehood, but a wicked man acts shamefully and disgracefully. — Proverbs 13:5", scriptureExplanation: "Righteousness hates lies. The upright detest deceit; the wicked embrace it. Our relationship with truth reveals our character." },
  { category: "jewish", scripture: "Righteousness guards him whose way is blameless, but sin overthrows the wicked. — Proverbs 13:6", scriptureExplanation: "Righteousness is protective. Integrity guards the upright; sin destroys the wicked. Character is both shield and vulnerability." },
  { category: "jewish", scripture: "One pretends to be rich, yet has nothing; another pretends to be poor, yet has great wealth. — Proverbs 13:7", scriptureExplanation: "Appearances deceive. Wealth is not always visible — some look rich but are empty; others seem poor but are rich. True wealth is hidden." },
  { category: "jewish", scripture: "The ransom of a man's life is his wealth, but a poor man hears no threat. — Proverbs 13:8", scriptureExplanation: "Wealth brings its own dangers. The rich face threats; the poor are overlooked. Each station has its own risks and freedoms." },
  { category: "jewish", scripture: "The light of the righteous rejoices, but the lamp of the wicked will be put out. — Proverbs 13:9", scriptureExplanation: "Righteousness shines; wickedness goes dark. The upright have lasting light; the wicked face extinction. Character determines illumination." },
  { category: "jewish", scripture: "By insolence comes nothing but strife, but with those who take advice is wisdom. — Proverbs 13:10", scriptureExplanation: "Pride causes conflict; humility brings wisdom. The arrogant fight; the teachable learn. Receptivity is the path to understanding." },
  { category: "jewish", scripture: "Wealth gained hastily will dwindle, but whoever gathers little by little will increase it. — Proverbs 13:11", scriptureExplanation: "Patience builds; haste loses. Quick riches evaporate; steady accumulation grows. The tortoise wins the financial race." },
  { category: "jewish", scripture: "Hope deferred makes the heart sick, but a desire fulfilled is a tree of life. — Proverbs 13:12", scriptureExplanation: "Fulfilled desire nourishes. Unmet hope depresses; realized longing gives life. Timing matters — both delay and fulfillment affect the heart." },
  { category: "jewish", scripture: "Whoever despises the word brings destruction on himself, but he who reveres the commandment will be rewarded. — Proverbs 13:13", scriptureExplanation: "Reverence for instruction brings reward. Despising guidance leads to ruin; respecting it leads to blessing. Our attitude toward wisdom shapes our outcome." },
  { category: "jewish", scripture: "The teaching of the wise is a fountain of life, that one may turn away from the snares of death. — Proverbs 13:14", scriptureExplanation: "Wise teaching gives life. It's a fountain — refreshing and protective. Learning from the wise keeps us from deadly traps." },
  { category: "jewish", scripture: "Good sense wins favor, but the way of the treacherous is their ruin. — Proverbs 13:15", scriptureExplanation: "Good judgment is attractive. Understanding earns favor; treachery earns ruin. How we think shapes how others receive us." },
  { category: "jewish", scripture: "In everything the prudent acts with knowledge, but a fool flaunts his folly. — Proverbs 13:16", scriptureExplanation: "The wise act thoughtfully; fools show off. Knowledge guides the prudent; folly is displayed by the foolish. Discretion vs. display." },
  { category: "jewish", scripture: "A wicked messenger falls into trouble, but a faithful envoy brings healing. — Proverbs 13:17", scriptureExplanation: "Faithfulness in communication brings healing. Unreliable messengers cause trouble; trustworthy ones mend. Reliability is restorative." },
  { category: "jewish", scripture: "Poverty and disgrace come to him who ignores instruction, but whoever heeds reproof is honored. — Proverbs 13:18", scriptureExplanation: "Accepting correction leads to honor. Ignoring instruction brings poverty and shame; heeding reproof brings respect. Teachability is rewarded." },
  { category: "jewish", scripture: "A desire fulfilled is sweet to the soul, but to turn away from evil is an abomination to fools. — Proverbs 13:19", scriptureExplanation: "Fulfilled desire is sweet — but fools hate turning from evil. The wise enjoy good things; the foolish resist what's good for them." },
  { category: "jewish", scripture: "Whoever walks with the wise becomes wise, but the companion of fools will suffer harm. — Proverbs 13:20", scriptureExplanation: "Companionship shapes character. Walk with the wise, become wise; hang with fools, get hurt. Our friends form our future." },
  { category: "jewish", scripture: "Disaster pursues sinners, but the righteous are rewarded with good. — Proverbs 13:21", scriptureExplanation: "Sin attracts disaster; righteousness attracts good. Not instant karma, but a pattern — our choices set a trajectory." },
  { category: "jewish", scripture: "The good leaves an inheritance to his children's children, but the sinner's wealth is laid up for the righteous. — Proverbs 13:22", scriptureExplanation: "Goodness has generational impact. The righteous leave a legacy; the wicked lose what they've stored. Character outlasts the person." },
  { category: "jewish", scripture: "The fallow ground of the poor yields much food, but it is swept away through injustice. — Proverbs 13:23", scriptureExplanation: "Injustice destroys abundance. Even when the poor produce, injustice takes it away. God cares about fair systems, not just personal morality." },
  { category: "jewish", scripture: "Whoever spares the rod hates his son, but he who loves him is diligent to discipline him. — Proverbs 13:24", scriptureExplanation: "Discipline is love. Not sparing correction shows care — the loving parent disciplines diligently. Guidance is a form of love." },
  { category: "jewish", scripture: "The righteous has enough to satisfy his appetite, but the belly of the wicked suffers want. — Proverbs 13:25", scriptureExplanation: "Righteousness brings satisfaction. The upright have enough; the wicked are always hungry. Contentment comes from character, not from abundance." },
  { category: "jewish", scripture: "Do not be envious of evil men, nor desire to be with them. — Proverbs 24:1", scriptureExplanation: "Don't envy or join the wicked. Their company is dangerous — not just their actions but their presence corrupts. Choose companions wisely." },
  { category: "jewish", scripture: "For their hearts devise violence, and their lips talk of trouble. — Proverbs 24:2", scriptureExplanation: "The wicked plan harm. Their hearts and mouths are both destructive — violence starts inside and comes out in speech." },
  { category: "jewish", scripture: "By wisdom a house is built, and by understanding it is established. — Proverbs 24:3", scriptureExplanation: "Wisdom builds. A home — and a life — is constructed by understanding. The foundation of any household is wisdom." },
  { category: "jewish", scripture: "By knowledge the rooms are filled with all precious and pleasant riches. — Proverbs 24:4", scriptureExplanation: "Knowledge fills the home. Not just material wealth but pleasant riches — understanding makes a house full and beautiful." },
  { category: "jewish", scripture: "A wise man is full of strength, and a man of knowledge enhances his might. — Proverbs 24:5", scriptureExplanation: "Wisdom is strength. Knowledge increases power — the wise are stronger than the merely muscular. Mind beats muscle." },
  { category: "jewish", scripture: "For by wise guidance you can wage your war, and in abundance of counselors there is victory. — Proverbs 24:6", scriptureExplanation: "Counsel leads to victory. Even battles are won by wisdom, not just force. Many advisors bring success." },
  { category: "jewish", scripture: "Wisdom is too high for a fool; in the gate he does not open his mouth. — Proverbs 24:7", scriptureExplanation: "Wisdom is beyond the foolish. They can't even participate in public discourse — the gate was the place of civic discussion. Folly silences." },
  { category: "jewish", scripture: "Whoever plans to do evil will be called a schemer. — Proverbs 24:8", scriptureExplanation: "Evil planning defines identity. Those who plot harm are known by their scheming — reputation follows intention." },
  { category: "jewish", scripture: "The devising of folly is sin, and the scoffer is an abomination to mankind. — Proverbs 24:9", scriptureExplanation: "Foolish planning is itself sin. It's not just the action but the devising — intention matters. Mockers are detested by everyone." },
  { category: "jewish", scripture: "If you faint in the day of adversity, your strength is small. — Proverbs 24:10", scriptureExplanation: "Adversity tests strength. If you collapse under pressure, your strength was insufficient — hardship reveals what we're made of." },
  { category: "jewish", scripture: "Rescue those who are being taken away to death; hold back those who are stumbling to the slaughter. — Proverbs 24:11", scriptureExplanation: "Rescue is a duty. We must save those in mortal danger — inaction is complicity. Justice requires intervention." },
  { category: "jewish", scripture: "If you say, 'Behold, we did not know this,' does not he who weighs the heart perceive it? — Proverbs 24:12a", scriptureExplanation: "Ignorance is no excuse. God knows our hearts — claiming we didn't know doesn't absolve us. He perceives both knowledge and motive." },
  { category: "jewish", scripture: "My son, eat honey, for it is good, and the drippings of the honeycomb are sweet to your taste. — Proverbs 24:13", scriptureExplanation: "Good things are to be enjoyed. Honey is sweet and good — God created pleasure. Enjoyment is not sin when received with gratitude." },
  { category: "jewish", scripture: "Know that wisdom is such to your soul; if you find it, there will be a future, and your hope will not be cut off. — Proverbs 24:14", scriptureExplanation: "Wisdom secures the future. Like honey is good for the body, wisdom is good for the soul — and it guarantees hope." },
  { category: "jewish", scripture: "Lie not in wait as a wicked man against the dwelling of the righteous; do no violence to his home. — Proverbs 24:15", scriptureExplanation: "Don't attack the righteous. Their home is not to be violated — God protects what belongs to the upright." },
  { category: "jewish", scripture: "For the righteous falls seven times and rises again, but the wicked stumble in times of calamity. — Proverbs 24:16", scriptureExplanation: "The righteous keep getting up. Falling is not the end — rising is. Seven falls, seven risings. Resilience defines the righteous." },
  { category: "jewish", scripture: "Do not rejoice when your enemy falls, and let not your heart be glad when he stumbles. — Proverbs 24:17", scriptureExplanation: "Don't gloat over enemies. Their downfall is not your victory — schadenfreude is forbidden. Compassion extends even to adversaries." },
  { category: "jewish", scripture: "Lest the Lord see it and be displeased and turn away his anger from him. — Proverbs 24:18", scriptureExplanation: "God notices gloating. He may redirect His anger from your enemy to you — rejoicing in others' pain provokes God's displeasure." },
  { category: "jewish", scripture: "Fret not yourself because of evildoers, and be not envious of the wicked. — Proverbs 24:19", scriptureExplanation: "Don't worry about the wicked. Their apparent success is temporary — envy of them is misplaced. Peace comes from trusting God's justice." },
  { category: "jewish", scripture: "For the evil man has no future; the lamp of the wicked will be put out. — Proverbs 24:20", scriptureExplanation: "The wicked have no future. Their light goes out — temporary success ends in darkness. Only the righteous have lasting hope." },
  { category: "jewish", scripture: "My son, fear the Lord and the king, and do not join with those who do otherwise. — Proverbs 24:21", scriptureExplanation: "Respect God and authority. Both divine and civil authority are to be honored — joining rebels brings destruction." },
  { category: "jewish", scripture: "For their disaster will rise suddenly, and who knows the ruin that will come from them both? — Proverbs 24:22", scriptureExplanation: "Rebellion brings sudden ruin. The destruction of the rebellious is unpredictable and swift — stay away from their path." },
  { category: "jewish", scripture: "These also are sayings of the wise: To show partiality in judging is not good. — Proverbs 24:23", scriptureExplanation: "Partiality is injustice. Favoritism in judgment is wrong — even the wise must be reminded. Fairness is a constant requirement." },
  { category: "jewish", scripture: "Whoever says to the wicked, 'You are in the right,' will be cursed by peoples, abhorred by nations. — Proverbs 24:24", scriptureExplanation: "Vindicating the guilty brings curse. Calling wrong 'right' is detested — justice requires naming evil for what it is." },
  { category: "jewish", scripture: "But those who rebuke the wicked will have delight, and a good blessing will come upon them. — Proverbs 24:25", scriptureExplanation: "Rebuking evil brings blessing. Confronting wickedness is rewarded — justice produces joy and divine favor." },
  { category: "jewish", scripture: "Whoever gives an honest answer kisses the lips. — Proverbs 24:26", scriptureExplanation: "Honesty is beautiful. A straightforward answer is like a kiss — truth-telling is an act of affection and respect." },
  { category: "jewish", scripture: "Prepare your work outside; get everything ready for yourself in the field, and after that build your house. — Proverbs 24:27", scriptureExplanation: "Order matters. Prepare your livelihood first, then build your home. Foundation before structure — provision before comfort." },
  { category: "jewish", scripture: "Be not a witness against your neighbor without cause, and do not deceive with your lips. — Proverbs 24:28", scriptureExplanation: "Don't testify falsely. Without cause, don't accuse — and don't lie. Truth in testimony is sacred." },
  { category: "jewish", scripture: "Do not say, 'I will do to him as he has done to me; I will pay the man back for what he has done.' — Proverbs 24:29", scriptureExplanation: "No personal vengeance. Don't repay evil with evil — leave justice to God. Retaliation is not our prerogative." },
  { category: "jewish", scripture: "I passed by the field of a sluggard, by the vineyard of a man lacking sense. — Proverbs 24:30", scriptureExplanation: "Observation teaches. The wise learn even from the fool's mistakes — seeing laziness in action is a lesson." },
  { category: "jewish", scripture: "And behold, it was all overgrown with thorns; the ground was covered with nettles, and its stone wall was broken down. — Proverbs 24:31", scriptureExplanation: "Neglect is visible. Laziness produces decay — thorns, weeds, broken walls. Inaction has consequences that are plain to see." },
  { category: "jewish", scripture: "Then I saw and considered it; I looked and received instruction. — Proverbs 24:32", scriptureExplanation: "Reflection brings learning. The wise observe, consider, and learn — even from ruin. Instruction comes through observation." },
  { category: "jewish", scripture: "A little sleep, a little slumber, a little folding of the hands to rest. — Proverbs 24:33", scriptureExplanation: "Small lazinesses compound. A little sleep here, a little rest there — small indulgences lead to big ruin. Neglect is incremental." },
  { category: "jewish", scripture: "And poverty will come upon you like a robber, and want like an armed man. — Proverbs 24:34", scriptureExplanation: "Poverty arrives suddenly through neglect. Like a thief, it comes when we're not looking — laziness invites ruin." },
  { category: "jewish", scripture: "Do not be afraid of sudden terror or of the ruin of the wicked when it comes. — Proverbs 3:25", scriptureExplanation: "Don't fear sudden disaster. The wicked's ruin is coming, but you need not fear it — God's protection is over the righteous." },
  { category: "jewish", scripture: "For the Lord will be your confidence and will keep your foot from being caught. — Proverbs 3:26", scriptureExplanation: "God is our confidence. He prevents stumbling — not by removing all obstacles, but by guiding our steps. Trust Him with your footing." },
  { category: "jewish", scripture: "Do not withhold good from those to whom it is due, when it is in your power to do it. — Proverbs 3:27 (alt.)", scriptureExplanation: "Do good when you can. If we have the power to help, we should — delay is a form of denial. Opportunity is a responsibility." },
  { category: "jewish", scripture: "Do not say to your neighbor, 'Go, and come again, tomorrow I will give it' when you have it with you. — Proverbs 3:28", scriptureExplanation: "Give when you have. Don't delay generosity — if you can help now, do it now. Procrastinated kindness is diminished kindness." },
  { category: "jewish", scripture: "Do not plan evil against your neighbor, who dwells trustingly beside you. — Proverbs 3:29 (alt.)", scriptureExplanation: "Trust is sacred. Our neighbor trusts us — don't betray that trust with malice. Community requires mutual safety." },
  { category: "jewish", scripture: "Do not contend with a man for no reason, when he has done you no harm. — Proverbs 3:30 (alt.)", scriptureExplanation: "Don't pick fights. Unnecessary conflict is foolish — if there's no harm, there's no reason to contend. Peace is the default." },
  { category: "jewish", scripture: "Do not envy a man of violence and do not choose any of his ways. — Proverbs 3:31 (alt.)", scriptureExplanation: "Don't admire the aggressive. Violence may seem powerful, but its path leads to destruction. Choose a different way." },
  { category: "jewish", scripture: "For the devious person is an abomination to the Lord, but the upright are in his confidence. — Proverbs 3:32 (alt.)", scriptureExplanation: "God shares secrets with the upright. Integrity brings intimacy with God — the honest enjoy His confidence." },
  { category: "jewish", scripture: "The Lord's curse is on the house of the wicked, but he blesses the dwelling of the righteous. — Proverbs 3:33 (alt.)", scriptureExplanation: "God's blessing follows righteousness. The home of the upright is blessed — character affects the household." },
  { category: "jewish", scripture: "Toward the scorners he is scornful, but to the humble he gives favor. — Proverbs 3:34 (alt.)", scriptureExplanation: "God matches our posture. The proud get scorn; the humble get grace. Our attitude toward God shapes His response to us." },
  { category: "jewish", scripture: "The wise will inherit honor, but fools get disgrace. — Proverbs 3:35 (alt.)", scriptureExplanation: "Wisdom leads to honor. The wise are respected; the foolish are shamed. Choices have consequences in reputation." },
  { category: "jewish", scripture: "Moses said to the people, 'Do not be afraid. Stand still and see the salvation of the Lord, which he will work for you today.' — Exodus 14:13", scriptureExplanation: "Stillness reveals God's salvation. Moses told the people to stop and watch — sometimes seeing God act requires stopping our own action." },
  { category: "jewish", scripture: "The Lord is a man of war; the Lord is his name. — Exodus 15:3", scriptureExplanation: "God fights for His people. He is not passive — He engages on behalf of those He loves. His power is active, not theoretical." },
  { category: "jewish", scripture: "The Lord is my strength and my song, and he has become my salvation. — Exodus 15:2a", scriptureExplanation: "God is both strength and song. He gives power and joy — salvation is not just rescue but celebration. He is the music of the saved." },
  { category: "jewish", scripture: "This is my God, and I will praise him, my father's God, and I will exalt him. — Exodus 15:2b", scriptureExplanation: "Personal and inherited faith. 'My God' and 'my father's God' — faith is both personal and generational. We praise what our fathers knew." },
  { category: "jewish", scripture: "Who is like you, O Lord, among the gods? Who is like you, majestic in holiness, awesome in glorious deeds, doing wonders? — Exodus 15:11", scriptureExplanation: "God is incomparable. No one is like Him — His holiness, His deeds, His wonders are unique. Worship is the response to His unmatched glory." },
  { category: "jewish", scripture: "You have led in your steadfast love the people whom you have redeemed; you have guided them by your strength to your holy abode. — Exodus 15:13", scriptureExplanation: "God leads with love and strength. He guides the redeemed — love and power together. His leadership is both tender and mighty." },
  { category: "jewish", scripture: "The Lord will reign forever and ever. — Exodus 15:18", scriptureExplanation: "God's reign is eternal. Not temporary, not contingent — forever and ever. His kingdom has no end." },
  { category: "jewish", scripture: "And he said, 'If you will diligently listen to the voice of the Lord your God, and do that which is right in his eyes, and give ear to his commandments and keep all his statutes, I will put none of the diseases on you that I put on the Egyptians.' — Exodus 15:26", scriptureExplanation: "Obedience brings protection. Listening, doing right, and keeping commands lead to health — God's instructions are for our good." },
  { category: "jewish", scripture: "For I am the Lord who heals you. — Exodus 15:26b", scriptureExplanation: "God is Healer. His name includes healing — He restores what's broken. Health flows from His character." },
  { category: "jewish", scripture: "The Lord your God is in your midst, a mighty one who will save; he will rejoice over you with gladness; he will quiet you by his love. — Zephaniah 3:17", scriptureExplanation: "God is not passive toward us — He actively delights. The image of God singing over His people is one of the most tender in Scripture." },
  { category: "jewish", scripture: "At that time I will bring you in, at the time when I gather you together; I will make you praised and famous in all the earth, when I restore your fortunes before your eyes. — Zephaniah 3:20", scriptureExplanation: "God's restoration is complete. He brings in, gathers, praises, and restores — His redemption is total and visible." },
  { category: "jewish", scripture: "The Lord your God is in your midst. — Zephaniah 3:17a", scriptureExplanation: "God's presence is the key. Not a distant deity but one who is 'in your midst' — near, present, active. Immanuel: God with us." },
  { category: "jewish", scripture: "He will rejoice over you with gladness. — Zephaniah 3:17b", scriptureExplanation: "God's emotion is joy. He doesn't just tolerate us — He rejoices. Gladness is His response to His people." },
  { category: "jewish", scripture: "He will quiet you by his love. — Zephaniah 3:17c", scriptureExplanation: "God's love calms. Like a parent soothing a child, His love quiets our anxious hearts. Peace is the effect of being loved." },
  { category: "jewish", scripture: "He will exult over you with loud singing. — Zephaniah 3:17d", scriptureExplanation: "God sings. The image is staggering — the Creator of the universe singing over His people. Worship is reciprocal: we sing to Him, He sings over us." },
  { category: "jewish", scripture: "Behold, at that time I will deal with all your oppressors. — Zephaniah 3:19a", scriptureExplanation: "God confronts oppressors. He doesn't ignore injustice — He deals with those who harm His people. Vindication is His work." },
  { category: "jewish", scripture: "And I will save the lame and gather the outcast, and I will change their shame into praise. — Zephaniah 3:19b", scriptureExplanation: "God transforms shame into praise. The marginalized — the lame, the outcast — are gathered and honored. His restoration reverses disgrace." },
  { category: "jewish", scripture: "For I know the plans I have for you, declares the Lord, plans for welfare and not for evil, to give you a future and a hope. — Jeremiah 29:11", scriptureExplanation: "Written to people in exile, this verse reminds us that even in uncertain seasons, God's purposes are still unfolding. Hope is about trusting the One who knows the outcome." },
  { category: "jewish", scripture: "Then you will call upon me and come and pray to me, and I will hear you. — Jeremiah 29:12", scriptureExplanation: "God responds to prayer. Calling, coming, praying — and hearing. God is not silent; He listens to those who seek Him." },
  { category: "jewish", scripture: "You will seek me and find me, when you seek me with all your heart. — Jeremiah 29:13", scriptureExplanation: "Wholehearted seeking finds God. Not casual inquiry but all-in pursuit — and the promise is that He will be found." },
  { category: "jewish", scripture: "I will be found by you, declares the Lord. — Jeremiah 29:14a", scriptureExplanation: "God makes Himself findable. He's not hiding — He wants to be found. The search is worth it because the Finder is willing." },
  { category: "jewish", scripture: "And I will restore your fortunes and gather you from all the nations and all the places where I have driven you, declares the Lord. — Jeremiah 29:14b", scriptureExplanation: "God restores and gathers. Even after exile, He brings back — His discipline is not His final word. Restoration follows." },
  { category: "jewish", scripture: "For I know the plans I have for you. — Jeremiah 29:11a", scriptureExplanation: "God's knowledge is personal. He knows — not just about us, but for us. His plans are specific, not generic." },
  { category: "jewish", scripture: "Plans for welfare and not for evil. — Jeremiah 29:11b", scriptureExplanation: "God's plans are good. Even when circumstances are hard, His intention is welfare — shalom, not harm. Trust His purpose." },
  { category: "jewish", scripture: "To give you a future and a hope. — Jeremiah 29:11c", scriptureExplanation: "God gives both future and hope. Not just survival but a future; not just endurance but hope. His plans are forward-looking and positive." },
  { category: "jewish", scripture: "Thus says the Lord: Let not the wise man boast in his wisdom, let not the mighty man boast in his might, let not the rich man boast in his riches. — Jeremiah 9:23", scriptureExplanation: "Don't boast in human achievements. Wisdom, power, wealth — none are worth boasting about. They're gifts, not accomplishments." },
  { category: "jewish", scripture: "But let him who boasts boast in this, that he understands and knows me, that I am the Lord who practices steadfast love, justice, and righteousness in the earth. — Jeremiah 9:24a", scriptureExplanation: "The only worthy boast: knowing God. Understanding Him — His love, justice, righteousness — is the true achievement. Relationship surpasses accomplishment." },
  { category: "jewish", scripture: "For in these things I delight, declares the Lord. — Jeremiah 9:24b", scriptureExplanation: "God delights in love, justice, and righteousness. These are His priorities — and when we know Him, they become ours too. Knowing God changes what we value." },
  { category: "jewish", scripture: "Return to the Lord your God, for you have stumbled because of your iniquity. — Hosea 14:1", scriptureExplanation: "Return is always possible. Stumbling is not the end — God invites us back. Repentance is the path home." },
  { category: "jewish", scripture: "Take with you words and return to the Lord; say to him, 'Take away all iniquity; accept what is good.' — Hosea 14:2a", scriptureExplanation: "Words of return. We come with words — not sacrifices, but speech. Honest confession is the offering God wants." },
  { category: "jewish", scripture: "I will heal their apostasy; I will love them freely, for my anger has turned from them. — Hosea 14:4", scriptureExplanation: "God heals and loves freely. His love is not reluctant — it flows freely once we return. Healing follows repentance." },
  { category: "jewish", scripture: "I will be like the dew to Israel; he shall blossom like the lily; he shall take root like the trees of Lebanon. — Hosea 14:5", scriptureExplanation: "God refreshes like dew. His presence causes growth — blossoming, rooting, flourishing. The result of return is life." },
  { category: "jewish", scripture: "His shoots shall spread out; his beauty shall be like the olive, and his fragrance like Lebanon. — Hosea 14:6", scriptureExplanation: "Restoration is beautiful. The returning people spread, become beautiful, and fragrant — God's renewal is both visible and attractive." },
  { category: "jewish", scripture: "They shall return and dwell beneath my shadow; they shall flourish like the grain; they shall blossom like the vine. — Hosea 14:7a", scriptureExplanation: "God's shadow is a place of flourishing. Under His protection, growth is natural — grain and vine both thrive in His shade." },
  { category: "jewish", scripture: "Their fragrance shall be like the wine of Lebanon. — Hosea 14:7b", scriptureExplanation: "Restoration has a fragrance. The renewed people carry a sweet aroma — transformation is noticeable, even attractive to others." },
  { category: "jewish", scripture: "O Ephraim, what have I to do with idols? It is I who answer and look after you. I am like an evergreen cypress; from me comes your fruit. — Hosea 14:8", scriptureExplanation: "God is the source of fruit. Idols are irrelevant — God Himself is the one who answers, cares, and produces. He is the evergreen, always alive." },
  { category: "jewish", scripture: "Whoever is wise, let him understand these things; whoever is discerning, let him know them; for the ways of the Lord are right, and the upright walk in them, but transgressors stumble. — Hosea 14:9", scriptureExplanation: "God's ways are right. The wise understand this — the upright walk in His paths, while transgressors stumble. Discernment leads to walking, not just knowing." },
  { category: "jewish", scripture: "But you, O Lord, are a God merciful and gracious, slow to anger and abounding in steadfast love and faithfulness. — Psalm 86:15", scriptureExplanation: "David appeals to God's character, not his own worthiness. Our hope is anchored in who God is, not in what we've done." },
  { category: "jewish", scripture: "Turn to me and be gracious to me, for I am lonely and afflicted. — Psalm 25:16", scriptureExplanation: "Honest prayer in loneliness. David doesn't hide his isolation — he brings it to God. Loneliness is met by turning to the Lord." },
  { category: "jewish", scripture: "The troubles of my heart are enlarged; bring me out of my distresses. — Psalm 25:17", scriptureExplanation: "Trouble grows; God rescues. David's heart is overwhelmed, but he asks for extraction — not just comfort but deliverance." },
  { category: "jewish", scripture: "Consider my affliction and my trouble, and forgive all my sins. — Psalm 25:18", scriptureExplanation: "God sees and forgives. David asks for both attention and pardon — God considers our pain and removes our guilt." },
  { category: "jewish", scripture: "Consider how many are my foes, and with what violent hatred they hate me. — Psalm 25:19", scriptureExplanation: "God counts our enemies. David asks God to notice the opposition — awareness of our struggle is the first step to deliverance." },
  { category: "jewish", scripture: "Oh, guard my soul, and deliver me! Let me not be put to shame, for I take refuge in you. — Psalm 25:20", scriptureExplanation: "God guards the soul. Taking refuge in Him means trusting Him with our innermost being — protection starts from within." },
  { category: "jewish", scripture: "May integrity and uprightness preserve me, for I wait for you. — Psalm 25:21", scriptureExplanation: "Character is protective. Integrity and uprightness guard us — not as earning, but as alignment. Waiting on God includes walking straight." },
  { category: "jewish", scripture: "Redeem Israel, O God, out of all his troubles. — Psalm 25:22", scriptureExplanation: "Prayer for community. David ends with a prayer for the whole people — personal need expands to communal care." },
  { category: "jewish", scripture: "The Lord is my light and my salvation; whom shall I fear? The Lord is the stronghold of my life; of whom shall I be afraid? — Psalm 27:1 (alt.)", scriptureExplanation: "Fear is answered by God's presence. Light, salvation, stronghold — three images that leave no room for fear. Trust displaces terror." },
  { category: "jewish", scripture: "Though an army encamp against me, my heart shall not fear; though war arise against me, yet I will be confident. — Psalm 27:3", scriptureExplanation: "Confidence in the face of overwhelming odds. David's trust is not naive — he sees the army, but his heart is steady because of God." },
  { category: "jewish", scripture: "One thing have I asked of the Lord, that will I seek after: that I may dwell in the house of the Lord all the days of my life. — Psalm 27:4 (alt.)", scriptureExplanation: "Single-minded pursuit. David's one request: God's presence. When we seek one thing, everything else finds its place." },
  { category: "jewish", scripture: "To gaze upon the beauty of the Lord and to inquire in his temple. — Psalm 27:4b", scriptureExplanation: "Beauty and inquiry together. Worship includes both awe and questions — gazing at God's beauty and seeking understanding in His presence." },
  { category: "jewish", scripture: "For he will hide me in his shelter in the day of trouble; he will conceal me under the cover of his tent; he will lift me high upon a rock. — Psalm 27:5 (alt.)", scriptureExplanation: "God's shelter is threefold: hidden, concealed, lifted. Each image speaks to a different kind of protection — secrecy, covering, elevation." },
  { category: "jewish", scripture: "And now my head shall be lifted up above my enemies all around me, and I will offer in his tent sacrifices with shouts of joy. — Psalm 27:6a", scriptureExplanation: "Victory leads to worship. When God lifts us up, our response is sacrifice and joy — gratitude is the language of the delivered." },
  { category: "jewish", scripture: "I will sing and make melody to the Lord. — Psalm 27:6b", scriptureExplanation: "Music is the natural response to deliverance. Singing and making melody — worship is both vocal and instrumental, both personal and joyful." },
  { category: "jewish", scripture: "Hear, O Lord, when I cry aloud; be gracious to me and answer me! — Psalm 27:7", scriptureExplanation: "Crying out is part of faith. David doesn't whisper — he cries aloud. Boldness in prayer is appropriate when approaching a gracious God." },
  { category: "jewish", scripture: "You have said, 'Seek my face.' My heart says to you, 'Your face, Lord, do I seek.' — Psalm 27:8", scriptureExplanation: "God invites; we respond. He says 'seek my face' — and the seeking heart says 'yes.' The initiative is God's; the response is ours." },
  { category: "jewish", scripture: "Hide not your face from me. Turn not your servant away in anger, O you who have been my help. — Psalm 27:9a", scriptureExplanation: "God's face is everything. David fears its absence more than any enemy — God's presence is his only true help." },
  { category: "jewish", scripture: "Cast me not off; forsake me not, O God of my salvation! — Psalm 27:9b", scriptureExplanation: "Abandonment is the deepest fear. David pleads not to be cast off — relationship with God is his salvation, not just rescue from trouble." },
  { category: "jewish", scripture: "For my father and my mother have forsaken me, but the Lord will take me in. — Psalm 27:10", scriptureExplanation: "God is better than family. Even if parents abandon us, God takes us in — His acceptance is more reliable than any human bond." },
  { category: "jewish", scripture: "Teach me your way, O Lord, and lead me on a level path because of my enemies. — Psalm 27:11", scriptureExplanation: "Guidance is for safety. David asks for a level path — not because life is easy, but because God's way keeps us from stumbling." },
  { category: "jewish", scripture: "Give me not up to the will of my adversaries; for false witnesses have risen against me, and they breathe out violence. — Psalm 27:12", scriptureExplanation: "God protects from false accusation. David faces liars and violence — he asks God not to hand him over. Truth is God's to vindicate." },
  { category: "jewish", scripture: "I believe that I shall look upon the goodness of the Lord in the land of the living! — Psalm 27:13", scriptureExplanation: "Hope is for this life, not just the next. David expects to see God's goodness now — in the land of the living. Hope is present tense." },
  { category: "jewish", scripture: "Wait for the Lord; be strong, and let your heart take courage; wait for the Lord! — Psalm 27:14 (alt.)", scriptureExplanation: "Courage and waiting are linked. It takes bravery to wait patiently — to trust God's timing instead of forcing our own. Waiting is active trust." },
];